h(2, id: "inlines") { Inlines }

p { Inlines appear in prose bodies: headings, paragraphs, callouts, table cells, footnote definitions, and the text parts of list items and quotes. }

p { Brace forms: code{b}, code{i}, code{strike}, code{code}, code{m}. Parenthesis forms: code{link}, code{fn}. }

h(3, id: "emphasis") { Emphasis }

codeblock(lang: "text") {!
p { b{bold} i{italic} strike{struck} }
!}

p { These nest. Depth is capped at 64; deeper input is a parse error. }

p { Rendered: b{bold} i{italic} strike{struck}. Combined: b{i{bold italic}}. }

h(3, id: "inline-code") { Inline code }

p { code{code{...}} reads bytes until the matching closing brace. Nested braces are counted. There are no inlines inside it. }

codeblock(lang: "text") {!
p { Use code{h(1) { Title }} at the top of a page. }
!}

p { HTML special characters in the body are escaped in output. }

h(3, id: "links") { Links }

codeblock(lang: "text") {!
p { See link("https://example.com") { the site }. }
!}

p { The URL is a string inside parentheses. The visible text is the brace body. An empty URL is a semantic error. Extra comma-separated string attributes after the URL are parsed and discarded. }

p { Example: link("https://example.com") { example.com }. }

h(3, id: "inline-math-ref") { Inline math and footnotes }

p { Inline math uses code{m{...}} and is described with math blocks. Footnote references use code{fn(id)} and are described with footnote blocks. }

p { An identifier that looks like an inline but is not one of the names above is ordinary text. }
